\lispPackageChapter{livermore/stocks-xcs}

The \texttt{livermore/stocks-xcs} package walks a price table and asks XCS
whether to be in the stock or in cash.  The situation is a vector of extrema
and $z$-score features from recent prices and volume.  Reward is chosen by
\texttt{*reward-method*}.

\section{Classes}

\lispClassSubsection{stocks-xcs-analyzer}

A subclass of \texttt{analyzer}.

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{table}] The price table.
\item[\texttt{initial-index}, \texttt{current-index}] The bar under
  consideration.
\item[\texttt{money}, \texttt{previous-money}] Cash marked to market.
\item[\texttt{previous-action}] The last action.
\end{description}

\lispClassSubsection{stocks-experiment}

An experiment that uses a \texttt{stocks-xcs-analyzer}.
\texttt{terminate?} is true at the last record or when money is not
positive.

\section{Methods}

\texttt{current-record} and \texttt{previous-record} are the bars at and
before \texttt{current-index}.

\texttt{should-have-bought-stock?} is true when today's adjusted close is
above yesterday's.  \texttt{buy-stock?} is true when the current action is
\texttt{:stock}, or when it is \texttt{:hold} and the previous action was
\texttt{:stock}.  \texttt{correct-action?} is true when that buy decision
matches whether the price rose.

\texttt{money-from-stock} marks to today's close as many shares as
yesterday's cash would buy.

\texttt{execute-action} advances one bar and, if buying, updates money.
\texttt{get-reward} returns a value chosen by \texttt{*reward-method*}
(\texttt{:money}, \texttt{:delta-money}, \texttt{:weighted-delta-money},
\texttt{:diff-money}, \texttt{:weighted-diff-money}, or
\texttt{:correctness}) and may print a periodic report.

\texttt{load-*table*} loads a ticker into \texttt{*table*}.
